PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L08U5.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":8,"cefr":"B2a","unit":5,"theme":"Exploration","essential_question":"Why do humans keep exploring the unknown?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1","title":"Launched in 1977, Voyager 1…: Reduced Relative Clauses and Participle Phrases","objectives":["Students will be able to shorten relative clauses with present participles (-ing) and past participles (-ed/-en).","Students will be able to begin sentences with participle phrases whose subject is clear and logical.","I can write \"Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still sending messages.\"","I can change \"scientists who work on the mission\" to \"scientists working on the mission\"."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من اختصار جمل الوصل باسم الفاعل (ing) واسم المفعول (ed/en).","سيتمكن الطلاب من بدء الجمل بعبارات اسم الفاعل أو المفعول ذات الفاعل الواضح المنطقي.","أستطيع أن أكتب Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still sending messages.","أستطيع أن أحوّل scientists who work on the mission إلى scientists working on the mission."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"voyage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɔɪədʒ/","definition":"a long journey, especially by sea or in space","arabic":"رحلة طويلة","example":"The voyage across the ocean took three months.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-W01"},{"word":"probe","pos":"noun","ipa":"/proʊb/","definition":"a spacecraft without people that is sent to explore space","arabic":"مسبار","example":"The probe sent photos of Saturn back to Earth.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-W02"},{"word":"orbit","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈɔrbət/","definition":"to move in a circle around a planet, moon or star","arabic":"يدور في مدار","example":"The International Space Station orbits the Earth every 90 minutes.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-W03"},{"word":"launch","pos":"verb","ipa":"/lɔntʃ/","definition":"to send a rocket or spacecraft into the sky or space","arabic":"يُطلق","example":"The rocket was launched from Florida.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"A reduced relative clause is a shorter way to write a relative clause. If the verb is active, use the -ing participle: scientists who work on the mission → scientists working on the mission. If the verb is passive, use the past participle: the probe which was built by NASA → the probe built by NASA.\nA participle phrase can also start a sentence and describe the subject that follows: Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still working. (Voyager 1 was launched.) Passing Jupiter, Voyager 1 sent photos. (Voyager 1 passed Jupiter.) Use Having + past participle for an action that happened before the main verb: Having visited Saturn, it headed out of the solar system.\nThe subject after the comma must be the one doing or receiving the action, or the modifier dangles (Level 7): (not: Launched in 1977, scientists still receive data.) (The scientists weren't launched!) Participles, gerunds and infinitives are called verbals: a gerund works as a noun (Exploring is exciting), a participle as an adjective (the exploring team; the lost probe), and an infinitive as a noun, adjective or adverb (We want to explore).\nwho/which + active verb → -ing (people living near the coast)\nwho/which + was/were + past participle → past participle (the photo taken in 1990)\nParticiple phrase, + the correct subject\nHaving + past participle = an earlier action","examples":["The photos sent by the probe amazed the world.","People living near the coast fear rising sea levels.","Built in 1960, the submersible Trieste reached the deepest point in the ocean.","Looking down at Earth, the astronaut felt very small.","Having trained for years, the crew was ready for the mission.","The rover is studying rocks formed billions of years ago.","Any spacecraft entering Earth's atmosphere gets extremely hot.","Exploring the deep ocean is more difficult than exploring space. (gerunds)"],"common_errors":["✗ The probe built by NASA it is still working. → ✓ The probe built by NASA is still working. (No extra subject pronoun.)","✗ The scientists worked on the mission are excited. → ✓ The scientists working on the mission are excited. (Active → -ing.)","✗ The photo taking in 1990 is famous. → ✓ The photo taken in 1990 is famous. (Passive → past participle.)","✗ Launched in 1977, scientists still receive its data. → ✓ Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 still sends data to scientists. (The subject must be the thing that was launched.)"],"arabic":"جملة الوصل المختصرة: إذا كان الفعل مبنيًّا للمعلوم نستخدم ing: scientists who work → scientists working، وإذا كان مبنيًّا للمجهول نستخدم التصريف الثالث: the probe which was built → the probe built.\nعبارة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول في أول الجملة تصف الفاعل الذي يليها: Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still working.\nHaving + التصريف الثالث لحدث سابق: Having visited Saturn, it headed out of the solar system.\nيجب أن يكون الفاعل بعد الفاصلة هو صاحب الفعل وإلا صار المُعدِّل معلّقًا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Launched in 1977, Voyager 1…: Reduced Relative Clauses and Participle Phrases”. Let’s learn reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U5-LS1 | Launched in 1977, Voyager 1…: Reduced Relative Clauses and Participle Phrases","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “The Little Spacecraft That Keeps Going”: Launched from Florida on September 5, 1977, the space probe Voyager 1 was designed to study Jupiter and Saturn. Nobody expected it to last more than five years. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Launched from Florida on September 5, 1977, the space probe Voyager 1 was designed to study Jupiter and Saturn. Nobody expected it to last more than five years.","visual":"Image GV-L08-U5-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A reduced relative clause is a shorter way to write a relative clause. If the verb is active, use the -ing participle: scientists who work on the mission → scientists working on the mission. If the verb is passive, use the past participle: the probe which was built by NASA → the probe built by NASA. A participle phrase can also start a sentence and describe the subject that follows: Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still working. (Voyager 1 was launched.) Passing Jupiter, Voyager 1 sent photos. (Voyager 1 passed Jupiter.) Use Having + past participle for an action that happened before the main verb: Having visited Saturn, it headed out of the solar system.","on_screen":"who/which + active verb → -ing (people living near the coast) • who/which + was/were + past participle → past participle (the photo taken in 1990) • Participle phrase, + the correct subject","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The photos sent by the probe amazed the world. People living near the coast fear rising sea levels. Built in 1960, the submersible Trieste reached the deepest point in the ocean.","on_screen":"The photos sent by the probe amazed the world. / People living near the coast fear rising sea levels. / Built in 1960, the submersible Trieste reached the deepest point in the ocean.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The probe built by NASA it is still working.” That’s not right. We say: “The probe built by NASA is still working.” No extra subject pronoun.","on_screen":"✗ The probe built by NASA it is still working.   ✓ The probe built by NASA is still working.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"The photo taken in 1990\" – did the photo take something? … Think! … No – it was taken (passive). Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still sending messages.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still sending messages.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","جملة الوصل المختصرة: إذا كان الفعل مبنيًّا للمعلوم نستخدم ing: scientists who work → scientists working، وإذا كان مبنيًّا للمجهول نستخدم التصريف الثالث: the probe which was built → the probe built.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The probe built by NASA it is still working. — الصحيح: The probe built by NASA is still working.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still sending messages."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Little Spacecraft That Keeps Going","body":"Launched from Florida on September 5, 1977, the space probe Voyager 1 was designed to study Jupiter and Saturn. Nobody expected it to last more than five years. Almost fifty years later, it is still sending messages to Earth.\nThe probe, built by a team of engineers at NASA, is about the size of a small car. Powered by a nuclear generator, it carries cameras and instruments developed in the 1970s—less powerful than a modern phone. Passing Jupiter in 1979, Voyager 1 sent back the first close-up photos of the planet's storms. Then, having visited Saturn in 1980, it headed out of the solar system.\nIn 1990, scientists working on the mission asked the probe to turn around and take a final photo of Earth. Taken from six billion kilometers away, the picture showed our planet as a tiny dot in a beam of sunlight. It became known as the \"Pale Blue Dot.\"\nIn 2012, Voyager 1 became the first human-made object to enter interstellar space, the region lying beyond the Sun's influence. Attached to its side is a golden record containing sounds and images from Earth, including greetings in many languages—among them, Arabic.\nToday, messages traveling from the probe take more than twenty hours to reach us. Its power is running low, but engineers hope to keep it working for a few more years.","word_count":225,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Scientists ___ on the Voyager mission asked the probe to take a photo of Earth.","options":["worked","working","are working","who working"],"answer":"working","explanation":"Active reduced clause → -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المعلومة ← ing."},{"q":"The photo ___ from six billion kilometers away became famous.","options":["was taken","taking","taken","which taken"],"answer":"taken","explanation":"Passive reduced clause → past participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المجهولة ← التصريف الثالث."},{"q":"___ from Florida in 1977, Voyager 1 is still sending messages.","options":["It was launched","Launching","Launched","Having launching"],"answer":"Launched","explanation":"Participle phrase at the start describing the subject.","explanation_ar":"العبارة في أول الجملة."},{"q":"___ Saturn in 1980, the probe headed out of the solar system.","options":["Having visit","Visited","Has visited","Having visited"],"answer":"Having visited","explanation":"Earlier action → Having + past participle.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"","options":["Passing Jupiter, Voyager 1 sent back amazing photos.","Passing Jupiter, scientists received amazing photos.","Passing Jupiter, amazing photos were sent back.","Passing Jupiter, the photos amazed scientists."],"answer":"Passing Jupiter, Voyager 1 sent back amazing photos.","explanation":"The subject must match the participle phrase.","explanation_ar":"تطابق الفاعل."},{"q":"The golden record ______ to the side of Voyager 1 contains greetings in fifty-five languages, including Arabic.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["which attached","attached","it is attached","attaching"],"answer":"attached","explanation":"Passive reduced clause → past participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المجهولة ← التصريف الثالث."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Reduce the relative clauses.","items":[{"q":"The rover which was sent to Mars in 2020 is called Perseverance.  (reduced clause)","options":[],"answer":"The rover sent to Mars in 2020 is called Perseverance.","explanation":"Passive reduced clause → past participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المجهولة ← التصريف الثالث."},{"q":"People who live near the coast must prepare for storms.  (reduced clause)","options":[],"answer":"People living near the coast must prepare for storms.","explanation":"Active reduced clause → -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المعلومة ← ing."},{"q":"The submersible which was designed by the team reached the ocean floor.  (reduced clause)","options":[],"answer":"The submersible designed by the team reached the ocean floor.","explanation":"Passive reduced clause → past participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المجهولة ← التصريف الثالث."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Combine with a participle phrase at the start.","items":[{"q":"The astronaut looked down at Earth.  +  She felt very small. (Looking…)","options":[],"answer":"Looking down at Earth, the astronaut felt very small.","explanation":"Participle phrase at the start describing the subject.","explanation_ar":"العبارة في أول الجملة."},{"q":"The crew had trained for two years.  +  They were ready for the mission. (Having…)","options":[],"answer":"Having trained for two years, the crew was ready for the mission.","explanation":"Earlier action → Having + past participle.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"The probe was powered by a nuclear generator.  +  It could work for decades. (Powered…)","options":[],"answer":"Powered by a nuclear generator, the probe could work for decades.","explanation":"Participle phrase at the start describing the subject.","explanation_ar":"العبارة في أول الجملة."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write concise sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with a noun + -ing phrase about space.","options":[],"answer":"Astronauts living on the space station exercise every day.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with a noun + past participle phrase about the ocean.","options":[],"answer":"Many ships lost at sea have never been found.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin a sentence with a past participle phrase.","options":[],"answer":"Discovered in 1930, Pluto was once called the ninth planet.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin a sentence with \"Having + past participle\".","options":[],"answer":"Having studied the map, the explorers set off at dawn.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with a gerund as the subject.","options":[],"answer":"Exploring caves can be dangerous.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Mission report.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences about a real or imaginary space mission using reduced relative clauses.","options":[],"answer":"The spacecraft carrying three astronauts left Earth at dawn. The samples collected on the Moon were sent to a lab.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences beginning with participle phrases.","options":[],"answer":"Approaching the Moon, the crew prepared to land. Having landed safely, they sent a message home.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check your sentences for dangling modifiers and fix one if needed.","options":[],"answer":"\"Approaching the Moon, the engines were switched off\" → \"Approaching the Moon, the crew switched off the engines.\"","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Grammar Hot Seat","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["One student from each team sits in the hot seat facing the class.","The teacher shows a cue card behind the student; the team gives a clue using reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical without saying the word.","The student in the hot seat guesses the cue word within 30 seconds.","Teams score 1 point for a correct guess and 1 point for a correct clue sentence. Rotate seats."],"data":[{"label":"Launched in 1977, …"},{"label":"Built by engineers, …"},{"label":"Passing Jupiter, …"},{"label":"Having visited Saturn, …"},{"label":"scientists working on…"},{"label":"the planet on which…"},{"label":"the astronaut to whom…"},{"label":"the team with which…"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Most of the water ___ on Earth is in the oceans.","options":["which found","found","finding","is found"],"answer":"found","explanation":"Passive reduced clause → past participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المجهولة ← التصريف الثالث."},{"q":"Marine biologists ___ the deep sea have discovered new species.","options":["who studying","studying","are studying","studied"],"answer":"studying","explanation":"Active reduced clause → -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المعلومة ← ing."},{"q":"___ at the stars for hours, the young astronomer forgot the time.","options":["Gazing","Gazes","Gazed","Having gaze"],"answer":"Gazing","explanation":"Participle phrase at the start describing the subject.","explanation_ar":"العبارة في أول الجملة."},{"q":"___ the summit, the climbers rested before going down.","options":["Having reach","Has reached","Reached them","Having reached"],"answer":"Having reached","explanation":"Earlier action → Having + past participle.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"","options":["Built in the 1960s, the museum visitors enjoy the submarine.","Built in the 1960s, visitors love the submarine museum.","Built in the 1960s, the submarine is now a museum.","Built in the 1960s, people now visit the submarine as a museum."],"answer":"Built in the 1960s, the submarine is now a museum.","explanation":"The subject must match the participle phrase.","explanation_ar":"تطابق الفاعل."},{"q":"In \"Exploring the ocean is expensive,\" the word \"Exploring\" is a ___.","options":["past participle","gerund","infinitive","main verb in the past"],"answer":"gerund","explanation":"Gerund, participle or infinitive.","explanation_ar":"المصادر والأسماء المشتقة."},{"q":"","options":["The rocks collecting by the rover are billions of years old.","The rocks collected by the rover they are billions of years old.","The rocks were collected by the rover are billions of years old.","The rocks collected by the rover are billions of years old."],"answer":"The rocks collected by the rover are billions of years old.","explanation":"Passive reduced clause → past participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المجهولة ← التصريف الثالث."},{"q":"Rayyanah Barnawi, the first Saudi woman ______ into space, conducted scientific experiments on the International Space Station in 2023.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["had traveled","travels","traveled","to travel"],"answer":"to travel","explanation":"Gerund, participle or infinitive.","explanation_ar":"المصادر والأسماء المشتقة."},{"q":"______ more than 400 kilometers above Earth, the International Space Station completes about sixteen orbits every day.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["Orbiting","Orbited","It orbits","Having orbit"],"answer":"Orbiting","explanation":"Participle phrase at the start describing the subject.","explanation_ar":"العبارة في أول الجملة."},{"q":"Which phrase can replace \"who are studying\"? \"Students who are studying astronomy visited the observatory.\"","options":["to study","have studied","studying","studied"],"answer":"studying","explanation":"Active reduced clause → -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المعلومة ← ing."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A flat-vector space scene: the small Voyager probe with its big dish antenna and a golden record on its side, flying past a striped Jupiter and a ringed Saturn; in the corner, a tiny pale blue dot in a beam of sunlight; a control room with engineers watching screens. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A flat-vector space scene: the small Voyager probe with its big dish antenna and a golden record on its side, flying past a striped Jupiter and a ringed Saturn; in the corner, a tiny pale blue dot in a beam of sunlight; a control room with engineers watching screens"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows voyage (a long journey, especially by sea or in space); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing voyage: a long journey, especially by sea or in space"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows probe (a spacecraft without people that is sent to explore space); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing probe: a spacecraft without people that is sent to explore space"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows orbit (to move in a circle around a planet, moon or star); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing orbit: to move in a circle around a planet, moon or star"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows launch (to send a rocket or spacecraft into the sky or space); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing launch: to send a rocket or spacecraft into the sky or space"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Grammar Hot Seat”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Grammar Hot Seat"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-W01","script":"voyage. voyage. The voyage across the ocean took three months.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-W02","script":"probe. probe. The probe sent photos of Saturn back to Earth.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-W03","script":"orbit. orbit. The International Space Station orbits the Earth every 90 minutes.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-W04","script":"launch. launch. The rocket was launched from Florida.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-T1","script":"Launched from Florida on September 5, 1977, the space probe Voyager 1 was designed to study Jupiter and Saturn. Nobody expected it to last more than five years. Almost fifty years later, it is still sending messages to Earth. The probe, built by a team of engineers at NASA, is about the size of a small car. Powered by a nuclear generator, it carries cameras and instruments developed in the 1970s—less powerful than a modern phone. Passing Jupiter in 1979, Voyager 1 sent back the first close-up photos of the planet's storms. Then, having visited Saturn in 1980, it headed out of the solar system. In 1990, scientists working on the mission asked the probe to turn around and take a final photo of Earth. Taken from six billion kilometers away, the picture showed our planet as a tiny dot in a beam of sunlight. It became known as the \"Pale Blue Dot.\" In 2012, Voyager 1 became the first human-made object to enter interstellar space, the region lying beyond the Sun's influence. Attached to its side is a golden record containing sounds and images from Earth, including greetings in many languages—among them, Arabic. Today, messages traveling from the probe take more than twenty hours to reach us. Its power is running low, but engineers hope to keep it working for a few more years.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Launched in 1977, Voyager 1…: Reduced Relative Clauses and Participle Phrases”. Let’s learn reduced relative clauses (the probe built by engineers; scientists working on the mission); participle phrases at the start of a sentence (Launched in 1977, …; Passing Jupiter, …; Having visited Saturn, …); verbals (gerunds, participles, infinitives); keeping the subject logical together. Listen to the start of “The Little Spacecraft That Keeps Going”: Launched from Florida on September 5, 1977, the space probe Voyager 1 was designed to study Jupiter and Saturn. Nobody expected it to last more than five years. Look at the green words. A reduced relative clause is a shorter way to write a relative clause. If the verb is active, use the -ing participle: scientists who work on the mission → scientists working on the mission. If the verb is passive, use the past participle: the probe which was built by NASA → the probe built by NASA. A participle phrase can also start a sentence and describe the subject that follows: Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still working. (Voyager 1 was launched.) Passing Jupiter, Voyager 1 sent photos. (Voyager 1 passed Jupiter.) Use Having + past participle for an action that happened before the main verb: Having visited Saturn, it headed out of the solar system. Here are some examples. The photos sent by the probe amazed the world. People living near the coast fear rising sea levels. Built in 1960, the submersible Trieste reached the deepest point in the ocean. Be careful! Some learners say: “The probe built by NASA it is still working.” That’s not right. We say: “The probe built by NASA is still working.” No extra subject pronoun. Quick check: \"The photo taken in 1990\" – did the photo take something? … Think! … No – it was taken (passive). Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 is still sending messages.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2","title":"The Planet on Which Life Was Found: Preposition + which / whom","objectives":["Students will be able to use preposition + which / whom in formal relative clauses.","Students will be able to transform informal relative clauses (the team she works with) into formal ones (the team with which she works).","I can write \"the submarine in which they dived\" and \"the scientist to whom the prize was given\".","I can avoid \"to who\" and double prepositions."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام حرف الجر + which / whom في جمل الوصل الرسمية.","سيتمكن الطلاب من تحويل جمل الوصل غير الرسمية (the team she works with) إلى رسمية (the team with which she works).","أستطيع أن أكتب the submarine in which they dived وthe scientist to whom the prize was given.","أستطيع أن أتجنّب to who وتكرار حرف الجر."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"spacecraft","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈspeɪˌskræft/","definition":"a vehicle that can travel in space","arabic":"مركبة فضائية","example":"The spacecraft landed safely on the Moon.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS2-W01"},{"word":"submersible","pos":"noun","ipa":"/səbˈmɝsɪbəl/","definition":"a small vessel that can go deep under water","arabic":"غوّاصة صغيرة / مركبة غاطسة","example":"The submersible reached the bottom of the ocean.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS2-W02"},{"word":"navigate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈnævəˌɡeɪt/","definition":"to find the right direction to travel","arabic":"يُبحر / يهتدي إلى الاتجاه","example":"Ancient sailors navigated by the stars.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS2-W03"},{"word":"terrain","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tɚˈeɪn/","definition":"an area of land and its physical features","arabic":"تضاريس","example":"The rover crossed rocky terrain on Mars.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"In formal English, we often put the preposition before the relative pronoun: the submarine in which they dived; the scientist to whom the prize was given; the team with which she works. Use which for things and whom (not who) after a preposition for people: (not: to who) → to whom.\nIn informal English, the preposition usually goes at the end, and we can use that or leave out the pronoun: the team (that) she works with; the man (who) I spoke to. Don't use the preposition twice: (not: the planet on which life exists on).\nin which / on which can often replace where: the cave in which (= where) they found the drawings. at which / during which / in which can refer to times: the moment at which the rocket launched. whose shows possession: the explorer whose ship sank.\nformal: noun + preposition + which (things) / whom (people)\ninformal: noun + (that) + … + preposition at the end\nno preposition twice • not \"to who\"\nin which = where • at which = when • whose = possession","examples":["The Trieste was the vessel in which Piccard and Walsh reached the Challenger Deep.","Prince Sultan bin Salman, to whom many young Saudis look up, flew into space in 1985.","Mars is the planet on which NASA's rovers are searching for signs of ancient life.","The engineers with whom she worked were from five countries.","The telescope through which we looked was over a hundred years old.","The year in which humans first walked on the Moon was 1969.","The explorer whose journals survived became famous.","The cave from which the strange sounds came was never fully explored."],"common_errors":["✗ The astronaut to who the medal was given is Saudi. → ✓ The astronaut to whom the medal was given is Saudi. (After a preposition → whom.)","✗ The planet on which life exists on is Earth. → ✓ The planet on which life exists is Earth. (Don't repeat the preposition.)","✗ The submarine in that they dived was tiny. → ✓ The submarine in which they dived was tiny. (Preposition + which (not that).)","✗ The team which she works is international. → ✓ The team with which she works is international. / The team she works with … (Don't forget the preposition.)"],"arabic":"في الأسلوب الرسمي نضع حرف الجر قبل ضمير الوصل: the submarine in which they dived، وthe scientist to whom the prize was given.\nنستخدم which لغير العاقل وwhom للعاقل بعد حرف الجر، ولا نقول to who.\nفي الأسلوب غير الرسمي يأتي حرف الجر في النهاية: the team she works with، ولا نكرر حرف الجر.\nin which تعني where، وat which تعني when، وwhose للملكية."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Planet on Which Life Was Found: Preposition + which / whom”. Let’s learn formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U5-LS2 | The Planet on Which Life Was Found: Preposition + which / whom","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: This is the lab in which the vaccine was developed. There was no one to whom they could send a signal. Who is the scientist with whom you worked?","on_screen":"This is the lab in which the vaccine was developed. | There was no one to whom they could send a signal. | Who is the scientist with whom you worked?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"In formal English, we often put the preposition before the relative pronoun: the submarine in which they dived; the scientist to whom the prize was given; the team with which she works. Use which for things and whom (not who) after a preposition for people: (not: to who) → to whom. In informal English, the preposition usually goes at the end, and we can use that or leave out the pronoun: the team (that) she works with; the man (who) I spoke to. Don't use the preposition twice: (not: the planet on which life exists on).","on_screen":"formal: noun + preposition + which (things) / whom (people) • informal: noun + (that) + … + preposition at the end • no preposition twice • not \"to who\"","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The Trieste was the vessel in which Piccard and Walsh reached the Challenger Deep. Prince Sultan bin Salman, to whom many young Saudis look up, flew into space in 1985. Mars is the planet on which NASA's rovers are searching for signs of ancient life.","on_screen":"The Trieste was the vessel in which Piccard and Walsh reached the Challenger Deep. / Prince Sultan bin Salman, to whom many young Saudis look up, flew into space in 1985. / Mars is the planet on which NASA's rovers are searching for signs of ancient life.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The astronaut to who the medal was given is Saudi.” That’s not right. We say: “The astronaut to whom the medal was given is Saudi.” After a preposition → whom.","on_screen":"✗ The astronaut to who the medal was given is Saudi.   ✓ The astronaut to whom the medal was given is Saudi.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"the scientist ___ the prize was given\" – to who or to whom? … Think! … *to whom*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"the submarine in which they dived\" and \"the scientist to whom the prize was given\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"the submarine in which they dived\" and \"the scientist to whom the prize was given\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","في الأسلوب الرسمي نضع حرف الجر قبل ضمير الوصل: the submarine in which they dived، وthe scientist to whom the prize was given.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The astronaut to who the medal was given is Saudi. — الصحيح: The astronaut to whom the medal was given is Saudi.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب the submarine in which they dived وthe scientist to whom the prize was given."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"The Trieste was the small vessel ___ Piccard and Walsh reached the deepest point of the ocean.","options":["in which","in that","which in","where in"],"answer":"in which","explanation":"Preposition + which for things.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + which."},{"q":"The astronaut ___ the medal was given thanked his team.","options":["to who","to which","to whom","whom to"],"answer":"to whom","explanation":"Preposition + whom for people.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + whom."},{"q":"The engineers ___ she worked came from five countries.","options":["with which","with who","with whom","whom with"],"answer":"with whom","explanation":"Preposition + whom for people.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + whom."},{"q":"","options":["The telescope through which we looked was very old.","The telescope through that we looked was very old.","The telescope which through we looked was very old.","The telescope through which we looked through was very old."],"answer":"The telescope through which we looked was very old.","explanation":"Don't repeat the preposition.","explanation_ar":"لا تكرار لحرف الجر."},{"q":"The year ___ humans first landed on the Moon was 1969.","options":["in whom","which in","on whom","in which"],"answer":"in which","explanation":"in which / on which = where; at which = when.","explanation_ar":"in which = where."},{"q":"Mars is the planet ______ NASA's rovers are now searching for signs of ancient life.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["on whom","on which","which","on that"],"answer":"on which","explanation":"Preposition + which for things.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + which."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with a preposition + which or whom. Word bank: on which, to whom, in which","items":[{"q":"The cave ______ the explorers found ancient drawings is now a museum.","options":[],"answer":"in which","explanation":"Preposition + which for things.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + which."},{"q":"The captain ______ the crew owed their lives was a hero.","options":[],"answer":"to whom","explanation":"Preposition + whom for people.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + whom."},{"q":"This is the rocket ______ the satellite was carried into orbit.","options":[],"answer":"on which","explanation":"Preposition + which for things.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + which."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Make the relative clauses formal.","items":[{"q":"The ship that they sailed on was 30 meters long.  (formal)","options":[],"answer":"The ship on which they sailed was 30 meters long.","explanation":"Preposition + which for things.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + which."},{"q":"The scientist who I spoke to works at KAUST.  (formal)","options":[],"answer":"The scientist to whom I spoke works at KAUST.","explanation":"Preposition + whom for people.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + whom."},{"q":"The team that she belongs to won the prize.  (formal)","options":[],"answer":"The team to which she belongs won the prize.","explanation":"Preposition + which for things.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + which."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write formal descriptions.","items":[{"q":"Describe a place with \"in which\".","options":[],"answer":"The library in which I study is quiet and bright.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a person with \"to whom\" or \"with whom\".","options":[],"answer":"My grandfather is the person to whom I tell all my secrets.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a time with \"at which\" or \"in which\".","options":[],"answer":"1985 was the year in which the first Arab astronaut went to space.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rewrite informally: \"the topic about which she wrote\".","options":[],"answer":"the topic she wrote about","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe an explorer with \"whose\".","options":[],"answer":"Ibn Battuta, whose journeys lasted almost thirty years, wrote a famous travel book.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Museum plaques.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 formal plaque texts for objects in an exploration museum.","options":[],"answer":"This is the diving suit in which the first Saudi marine biologist explored the Red Sea. This is the compass with which sailors navigated the Indian Ocean.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a plaque about a person with \"to whom\" or \"by whom\".","options":[],"answer":"The explorer by whom this map was drawn traveled for twenty years.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"in which\" meaning \"where\".","options":[],"answer":"The station in which the astronauts live orbits Earth every ninety minutes.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Sentence Builder Race","mechanic":"sentence_builder","rules":["Each team receives the same tile set.","In 5 minutes, build as many different correct sentences as possible using formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions.","Write each sentence on the team sheet.","Swap sheets with another team to check. One point per correct sentence; no points for repeats."],"data":[{"cue":"the probe / launch / 1977 (Launched…)"},{"cue":"the photo / take / six billion km away (Taken…)"},{"cue":"scientists / work / on the mission (working…)"},{"cue":"having / visit / Saturn / it / leave the solar system"},{"cue":"the ocean floor / on which / they / find / strange creatures"},{"cue":"the astronaut / to whom / the medal / was given"},{"cue":"the submarine / in which / they / dive"},{"cue":"the team / with which / she / works"},{"cue":"looking down at Earth / the astronaut / feel / amazed"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The submarine ___ they traveled had room for only three people.","options":["in which","which in","in whom","in that"],"answer":"in which","explanation":"Preposition + which for things.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + which."},{"q":"The professor ___ we sent our questions replied the next day.","options":["whom to","to whom","to who","to which"],"answer":"to whom","explanation":"Preposition + whom for people.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + whom."},{"q":"","options":["The planet we are learning about about has two moons.","The planet we are learning about has two moons.","The planet about that we are learning has two moons.","The planet about we are learning has two moons."],"answer":"The planet we are learning about has two moons.","explanation":"Preposition at the end (informal).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."},{"q":"The moment ___ the rocket left the ground, everyone cheered.","options":["at whom","which at","at which","in whom"],"answer":"at which","explanation":"in which / on which = where; at which = when.","explanation_ar":"in which = where."},{"q":"The explorer ___ ship was trapped in the ice survived for months.","options":["whose","who's","which","whom"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"whose for possession.","explanation_ar":"whose."},{"q":"","options":["The island on which they landed on was uninhabited.","The island which on they landed was uninhabited.","The island on that they landed was uninhabited.","The island on which they landed was uninhabited."],"answer":"The island on which they landed was uninhabited.","explanation":"Don't repeat the preposition.","explanation_ar":"لا تكرار لحرف الجر."},{"q":"The people ___ we traveled were very experienced.","options":["with who","with whom","whom with","with which"],"answer":"with whom","explanation":"Preposition + whom for people.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + whom."},{"q":"The Hubble Space Telescope, ______ astronomers have discovered thousands of distant galaxies, was launched in 1990.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["through whom","through that","which","through which"],"answer":"through which","explanation":"Preposition + which for things.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + which."},{"q":"Prince Sultan bin Salman, ______ the honor of being the first Arab in space belongs, flew on the Space Shuttle Discovery in 1985.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["to which","to who","to whom","whom"],"answer":"to whom","explanation":"Preposition + whom for people.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + whom."},{"q":"Which version would suit an academic article best?","options":["The scientist with whom I worked was patient.","The scientist I worked with was patient.","The scientist that I worked with was patient.","The scientist who I worked with was patient."],"answer":"The scientist with whom I worked was patient.","explanation":"Preposition at the end (informal).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A museum of exploration: a deep-sea submersible on display with a plaque \"The vessel in which Piccard and Walsh reached the deepest point of the ocean\"; a medal case \"The astronaut to whom this medal was awarded\"; Noura and Adam reading the plaques. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A museum of exploration: a deep-sea submersible on display with a plaque \"The vessel in which Piccard and Walsh reached the deepest point of the ocean\"; a medal case \"The astronaut to whom this medal was awarded\"; Noura and Adam reading the plaques"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions: a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Exploration”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which)"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows spacecraft (a vehicle that can travel in space); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing spacecraft: a vehicle that can travel in space"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows submersible (a small vessel that can go deep under water); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing submersible: a small vessel that can go deep under water"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows navigate (to find the right direction to travel); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing navigate: to find the right direction to travel"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows terrain (an area of land and its physical features); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing terrain: an area of land and its physical features"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Sentence Builder Race”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Sentence Builder Race"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS2-W01","script":"spacecraft. spacecraft. The spacecraft landed safely on the Moon.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS2-W02","script":"submersible. submersible. The submersible reached the bottom of the ocean.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS2-W03","script":"navigate. navigate. Ancient sailors navigated by the stars.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS2-W04","script":"terrain. terrain. The rover crossed rocky terrain on Mars.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Planet on Which Life Was Found: Preposition + which / whom”. Let’s learn formal relative clauses with a preposition before which/whom (on which, in which, to whom, with whom, for which); informal alternative with the preposition at the end; in which = where, at which = when; whose; no double prepositions together. Remember these patterns: This is the lab in which the vaccine was developed. There was no one to whom they could send a signal. Who is the scientist with whom you worked? In formal English, we often put the preposition before the relative pronoun: the submarine in which they dived; the scientist to whom the prize was given; the team with which she works. Use which for things and whom (not who) after a preposition for people: (not: to who) → to whom. In informal English, the preposition usually goes at the end, and we can use that or leave out the pronoun: the team (that) she works with; the man (who) I spoke to. Don't use the preposition twice: (not: the planet on which life exists on). Here are some examples. The Trieste was the vessel in which Piccard and Walsh reached the Challenger Deep. Prince Sultan bin Salman, to whom many young Saudis look up, flew into space in 1985. Mars is the planet on which NASA's rovers are searching for signs of ancient life. Be careful! Some learners say: “The astronaut to who the medal was given is Saudi.” That’s not right. We say: “The astronaut to whom the medal was given is Saudi.” After a preposition → whom. Quick check: \"the scientist ___ the prize was given\" – to who or to whom? … Think! … *to whom*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"the submarine in which they dived\" and \"the scientist to whom the prize was given\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3","title":"Astronaut, Aquanaut, Terrain: Exploration Words and Greek/Latin Roots","objectives":["Students will be able to use exploration vocabulary accurately.","Students will be able to use Greek and Latin roots to work out the meanings of words such as aquanaut, terrain and hydrothermal.","I can explain that an \"astronaut\" is a \"star sailor\".","I can use exploration words like orbit, terrain and uncharted."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام مفردات الاستكشاف بدقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام الجذور اليونانية واللاتينية لاستنتاج معاني كلمات مثل aquanaut وterrain وhydrothermal.","أستطيع أن أشرح أن astronaut تعني «بحّار النجوم».","أستطيع أن أستخدم كلمات الاستكشاف مثل orbit وterrain وuncharted."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"lunar","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈlunɚ/","definition":"connected with the Moon","arabic":"قمري","example":"The lunar surface is covered in dust.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W01"},{"word":"marine","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/mɚˈin/","definition":"connected with the sea","arabic":"بحري","example":"Marine biologists study life in the ocean.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W02"},{"word":"uncharted","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ənˈtʃɑrtɪd/","definition":"not yet explored or shown on a map","arabic":"غير مستكشَف / مجهول","example":"Much of the deep ocean is still uncharted.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W03"},{"word":"frontier","pos":"noun","ipa":"/frənˈtɪr/","definition":"the edge of what is known or explored","arabic":"آفاق جديدة / تخوم","example":"Space is often called the final frontier.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W04"},{"word":"astronaut","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæstrəˌnɑt/","definition":"a person who travels into space","arabic":"رائد فضاء","example":"The astronaut spent six months on the space station.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W05"},{"word":"atmosphere","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈætməˌsfɪr/","definition":"the layer of gases around a planet","arabic":"الغلاف الجوي","example":"Mars has a very thin atmosphere.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W06"},{"word":"hostile","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈhɑstəl/","definition":"very difficult or dangerous to live in","arabic":"معادٍ / قاسٍ","example":"The deep sea is a hostile environment for humans.","image_id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Exploration words: voyage, probe, orbit, launch, spacecraft, submersible, navigate, terrain, lunar, marine, uncharted, frontier, astronaut, atmosphere, hostile.\nRoots unlock meaning: naut = sailor (astronaut, aquanaut, nautical); astro / aster = star (astronomy, asterisk); aqua / hydro = water (aquarium, hydropower); terra / geo = earth, land (terrain, territory, geology); mar = sea (marine, submarine); luna = moon (lunar); sol = sun (solar); port = carry (transport); spect / vis = see (inspect, visible); meter = measure (thermometer).\nWord families: explore → exploration → explorer → exploratory; navigate → navigation → navigator; observe → observation → observatory; orbit (n/v) → orbital; atmosphere → atmospheric.\nWord families: explore: exploration (n), explorer (n), exploratory (adj) | navigate: navigation (n), navigator (n) | observe: observation (n), observatory (n), observant (adj) | orbit: orbital (adj) | atmosphere: atmospheric (adj) | survive: survival (n), survivor (n)","examples":["The probe sent back data about Jupiter's atmosphere.","The rocket was launched at dawn.","The rover crossed the rocky terrain slowly.","Lunar soil is very fine and sticky.","Marine scientists are mapping the Red Sea's coral reefs.","More than 80% of the ocean is still uncharted.","Mars is a hostile place for humans: cold, dry and without oxygen.","An aquanaut lives and works underwater for days."],"common_errors":["✗ The astronaut made a big explore of Mars. → ✓ The astronaut made a big exploration of Mars. / The astronauts explored Mars. (After a big → noun.)","✗ The moon surface is grey. → ✓ The lunar surface is grey. / The Moon's surface is grey. (Adjective → lunar.)","✗ Sailors navigated by the moon. (meaning: they found their way) → ✓ Sailors navigated by the stars. (Ancient sailors used stars (and the Sun) to navigate.)","✗ The spaceship was in orbit around of the Earth. → ✓ The spaceship was in orbit around the Earth. (in orbit around (no of).)"],"arabic":"مفردات الاستكشاف: probe (مسبار) وorbit (يدور في مدار) وlaunch (يُطلق) وterrain (تضاريس) وuncharted (غير مستكشَف) وhostile (قاسٍ).\nالجذور: naut (بحّار)، astro (نجم)، aqua / hydro (ماء)، terra / geo (أرض)، mar (بحر)، luna (قمر)، sol (شمس)، port (يحمل)، spect / vis (يرى)، meter (يقيس).\nastronaut تعني حرفيًّا «بحّار النجوم»، وaquanaut «بحّار الماء».\nعائلات الكلمات: explore ← exploration ← explorer، وnavigate ← navigation ← navigator."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Astronaut, Aquanaut, Terrain: Exploration Words and Greek/Latin Roots”. Let’s learn exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U5-LS3 | Astronaut, Aquanaut, Terrain: Exploration Words and Greek/Latin Roots","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. lunar: connected with the Moon. marine: connected with the sea. uncharted: not yet explored or shown on a map.","on_screen":"lunar | marine | uncharted","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Exploration words: voyage, probe, orbit, launch, spacecraft, submersible, navigate, terrain, lunar, marine, uncharted, frontier, astronaut, atmosphere, hostile. Roots unlock meaning: naut = sailor (astronaut, aquanaut, nautical); astro / aster = star (astronomy, asterisk); aqua / hydro = water (aquarium, hydropower); terra / geo = earth, land (terrain, territory, geology); mar = sea (marine, submarine); luna = moon (lunar); sol = sun (solar); port = carry (transport); spect / vis = see (inspect, visible); meter = measure (thermometer).","on_screen":"exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The probe sent back data about Jupiter's atmosphere. The rocket was launched at dawn. The rover crossed the rocky terrain slowly.","on_screen":"The probe sent back data about Jupiter's atmosphere. / The rocket was launched at dawn. / The rover crossed the rocky terrain slowly.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The astronaut made a big explore of Mars.” That’s not right. We say: “The astronaut made a big exploration of Mars. / The astronauts explored Mars.” After a big → noun.","on_screen":"✗ The astronaut made a big explore of Mars.   ✓ The astronaut made a big exploration of Mars. / The astronauts explored Mars.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: What does \"naut\" mean in \"astronaut\"? … Think! … sailor. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain that an \"astronaut\" is a \"star sailor\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain that an \"astronaut\" is a \"star sailor\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","مفردات الاستكشاف: probe (مسبار) وorbit (يدور في مدار) وlaunch (يُطلق) وterrain (تضاريس) وuncharted (غير مستكشَف) وhostile (قاسٍ).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The astronaut made a big explore of Mars. — الصحيح: The astronaut made a big exploration of Mars. / The astronauts explored Mars.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح أن astronaut تعني «بحّار النجوم»."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"The rocket was ___ from the launch pad at 6 a.m.","options":["launched","uncharted","navigated","orbited"],"answer":"launched","explanation":"Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The root \"naut\" in \"astronaut\" means ___.","options":["star","water","earth","sailor"],"answer":"sailor","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root.","explanation_ar":"الجذر."},{"q":"The rover moved slowly across the rocky Martian ___.","options":["voyage","terrain","frontier","atmosphere"],"answer":"terrain","explanation":"Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Ancient sailors used the stars for ___.","options":["navigate","navigation","navigator","navigational"],"answer":"navigation","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"\"Hydro\" in \"hydropower\" means ___.","options":["moon","sun","water","land"],"answer":"water","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root.","explanation_ar":"الجذر."},{"q":"The deep ocean, with its crushing pressure, total darkness and freezing temperatures, is one of the most ______ environments on Earth for human explorers.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["hostile","familiar","crowded","welcoming"],"answer":"hostile","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"aquanaut","options":["a person who lives and works underwater","a building with telescopes for studying the sky","to carry people or goods from one place to another","a ship that travels under the sea","connected with the Sun","a tool that measures temperature"],"answer":"a person who lives and works underwater","explanation":"aquanaut → a person who lives and works underwater. Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"submarine","options":["a person who lives and works underwater","a building with telescopes for studying the sky","to carry people or goods from one place to another","a ship that travels under the sea","connected with the Sun","a tool that measures temperature"],"answer":"a ship that travels under the sea","explanation":"submarine → a ship that travels under the sea. Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"solar","options":["a person who lives and works underwater","a building with telescopes for studying the sky","to carry people or goods from one place to another","a ship that travels under the sea","connected with the Sun","a tool that measures temperature"],"answer":"connected with the Sun","explanation":"solar → connected with the Sun. Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"observatory","options":["a person who lives and works underwater","a building with telescopes for studying the sky","to carry people or goods from one place to another","a ship that travels under the sea","connected with the Sun","a tool that measures temperature"],"answer":"a building with telescopes for studying the sky","explanation":"observatory → a building with telescopes for studying the sky. Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"transport","options":["a person who lives and works underwater","a building with telescopes for studying the sky","to carry people or goods from one place to another","a ship that travels under the sea","connected with the Sun","a tool that measures temperature"],"answer":"to carry people or goods from one place to another","explanation":"transport → to carry people or goods from one place to another. Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"thermometer","options":["a person who lives and works underwater","a building with telescopes for studying the sky","to carry people or goods from one place to another","a ship that travels under the sea","connected with the Sun","a tool that measures temperature"],"answer":"a tool that measures temperature","explanation":"thermometer → a tool that measures temperature. Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Explore with words.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with \"orbit\".","options":[],"answer":"The Moon orbits the Earth about once a month.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"uncharted\".","options":[],"answer":"Some parts of the Empty Quarter were uncharted until the 20th century.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Find a word with \"geo\" or \"terra\" and explain its meaning.","options":[],"answer":"Territory – an area of land that belongs to a country.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"atmosphere\".","options":[],"answer":"Earth's atmosphere protects us from harmful rays.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Would you rather be an astronaut or an aquanaut? Why?","options":[],"answer":"I'd rather be an aquanaut because I love marine life.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Explorer's log.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 log entries for a voyage (space or ocean) using 3 words from the unit.","options":[],"answer":"Day 1: The spacecraft was launched successfully. Day 4: We entered lunar orbit. Day 6: The terrain below looks grey and hostile.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences with word-family forms (exploration, explorer, navigation, observation).","options":[],"answer":"Space exploration is expensive. Careful observation helped the explorers survive.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Invent a new \"-naut\" word and define it.","options":[],"answer":"Cavenaut – a person who explores deep caves.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Family Tree","mechanic":"drag_and_drop","rules":["Teams take turns drawing a word card.","Place the card on the correct branch of the tree and say a sentence with it.","Correct placement and sentence = 2 points.","The team with the most points when the tree is full wins."],"data":[{"word":"exploration","category":"noun","family":"explore"},{"word":"explorer","category":"noun","family":"explore"},{"word":"exploratory","category":"adjective","family":"explore"},{"word":"navigation","category":"noun","family":"navigate"},{"word":"navigator","category":"noun","family":"navigate"},{"word":"observation","category":"noun","family":"observe"},{"word":"observatory","category":"noun","family":"observe"},{"word":"observant","category":"adjective","family":"observe"},{"word":"orbital","category":"adjective","family":"orbit"},{"word":"atmospheric","category":"adjective","family":"atmosphere"},{"word":"survival","category":"noun","family":"survive"},{"word":"survivor","category":"noun","family":"survive"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The space ___ sent back photos of Pluto's icy surface.","options":["probe","terrain","frontier","orbit"],"answer":"probe","explanation":"Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"\"Mar\" in \"marine\" means ___.","options":["earth","sea","star","sun"],"answer":"sea","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root.","explanation_ar":"الجذر."},{"q":"The ___ of the Moon is covered in fine grey dust.","options":["terrain lunar","lunar surface","solar surface","marine surface"],"answer":"lunar surface","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root.","explanation_ar":"الجذر."},{"q":"The ___ of the deep ocean has only just begun.","options":["exploration","exploratory","explore","explorer"],"answer":"exploration","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Ibn Battuta was a famous ___ who traveled through Africa and Asia.","options":["exploration","exploratory","explorer","explore"],"answer":"explorer","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"The International Space Station has an ___ speed of about 28,000 kilometers per hour.","options":["orbiting it","orbitally","orbit","orbital"],"answer":"orbital","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"The ship's long ___ across the Indian Ocean took several months.","options":["launch","terrain","voyage","probe"],"answer":"voyage","explanation":"Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Space is often described as the final ___ – the edge of human knowledge.","options":["terrain","voyage","frontier","submersible"],"answer":"frontier","explanation":"Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Because so little of the seafloor has been mapped in detail, scientists describe much of the deep ocean as ______.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["polluted","well-known","overcrowded","uncharted"],"answer":"uncharted","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The ______ studied the night sky so carefully that she noticed a tiny new comet that others had missed.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["careless sailor","hostile explorer","uncharted scientist","observant astronomer"],"answer":"observant astronomer","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A split poster of space and ocean: an astronaut floating near a space station and an aquanaut in an underwater base; root labels (astro, naut, aqua, hydro, terra, mar, luna, sol) connected with lines to example words. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A split poster of space and ocean: an astronaut floating near a space station and an aquanaut in an underwater base; root labels (astro, naut, aqua, hydro, terra, mar, luna, sol) connected with lines to example words"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows lunar (connected with the Moon); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing lunar: connected with the Moon"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows marine (connected with the sea); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing marine: connected with the sea"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows uncharted (not yet explored or shown on a map); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing uncharted: not yet explored or shown on a map"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows frontier (the edge of what is known or explored); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing frontier: the edge of what is known or explored"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows astronaut (a person who travels into space); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing astronaut: a person who travels into space"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows atmosphere (the layer of gases around a planet); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing atmosphere: the layer of gases around a planet"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows hostile (very difficult or dangerous to live in); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing hostile: very difficult or dangerous to live in"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Family Tree”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Family Tree"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W01","script":"lunar. lunar. The lunar surface is covered in dust.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W02","script":"marine. marine. Marine biologists study life in the ocean.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W03","script":"uncharted. uncharted. Much of the deep ocean is still uncharted.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W04","script":"frontier. frontier. Space is often called the final frontier.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W05","script":"astronaut. astronaut. The astronaut spent six months on the space station.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W06","script":"atmosphere. atmosphere. Mars has a very thin atmosphere.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-W07","script":"hostile. hostile. The deep sea is a hostile environment for humans.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Astronaut, Aquanaut, Terrain: Exploration Words and Greek/Latin Roots”. Let’s learn exploration vocabulary; Greek/Latin roots (naut = sailor, astro/aster = star, aqua/hydro = water, terra/geo = earth, mar = sea, luna = moon, sol = sun, port = carry, spect/vis = see, meter = measure); word families (explore → exploration → explorer) together. Meet some new words. lunar: connected with the Moon. marine: connected with the sea. uncharted: not yet explored or shown on a map. Exploration words: voyage, probe, orbit, launch, spacecraft, submersible, navigate, terrain, lunar, marine, uncharted, frontier, astronaut, atmosphere, hostile. Roots unlock meaning: naut = sailor (astronaut, aquanaut, nautical); astro / aster = star (astronomy, asterisk); aqua / hydro = water (aquarium, hydropower); terra / geo = earth, land (terrain, territory, geology); mar = sea (marine, submarine); luna = moon (lunar); sol = sun (solar); port = carry (transport); spect / vis = see (inspect, visible); meter = measure (thermometer). Here are some examples. The probe sent back data about Jupiter's atmosphere. The rocket was launched at dawn. The rover crossed the rocky terrain slowly. Be careful! Some learners say: “The astronaut made a big explore of Mars.” That’s not right. We say: “The astronaut made a big exploration of Mars. / The astronauts explored Mars.” After a big → noun. Quick check: What does \"naut\" mean in \"astronaut\"? … Think! … sailor. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain that an \"astronaut\" is a \"star sailor\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS4","title":"The Deepest Point—Nearly 11,000 Meters…: Dashes and Ellipses","objectives":["Students will be able to use dashes to show a break or to set off nonessential information, always in matching pairs in the middle of a sentence.","Students will be able to use ellipses to show omitted words in quotations and pauses in dialogue.","I can write \"The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.\"","I can shorten a quotation with an ellipsis (…)."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام الشرطة لإظهار انقطاع أو لفصل معلومة غير ضرورية، وفي أزواج متطابقة وسط الجملة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام علامة الحذف لإظهار الكلمات المحذوفة من الاقتباسات والتوقف في الحوار.","أستطيع أن أكتب The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.","أستطيع أن أختصر اقتباسًا بعلامة الحذف (…)."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"voyage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɔɪədʒ/","definition":"a long journey, especially by sea or in space","arabic":"رحلة طويلة","example":"The voyage across the ocean took three months.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"probe","pos":"noun","ipa":"/proʊb/","definition":"a spacecraft without people that is sent to explore space","arabic":"مسبار","example":"The probe sent photos of Saturn back to Earth.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"orbit","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈɔrbət/","definition":"to move in a circle around a planet, moon or star","arabic":"يدور في مدار","example":"The International Space Station orbits the Earth every 90 minutes.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"launch","pos":"verb","ipa":"/lɔntʃ/","definition":"to send a rocket or spacecraft into the sky or space","arabic":"يُطلق","example":"The rocket was launched from Florida.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"spacecraft","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈspeɪˌskræft/","definition":"a vehicle that can travel in space","arabic":"مركبة فضائية","example":"The spacecraft landed safely on the Moon.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"submersible","pos":"noun","ipa":"/səbˈmɝsɪbəl/","definition":"a small vessel that can go deep under water","arabic":"غوّاصة صغيرة / مركبة غاطسة","example":"The submersible reached the bottom of the ocean.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"A dash (—) shows a strong break in a sentence. Use one dash before an explanation, a list or an emphatic ending: They needed one thing to survive—oxygen. Use a pair of dashes around nonessential information in the middle of a sentence: Voyager 1—launched in 1977—is still working.\nUse matching punctuation: a dash must be closed by a dash, not a comma ((not: The probe—launched in 1977, is still working.)). This is a common SAT Boundaries question. In American English, the long dash usually has no spaces.\nAn ellipsis (…) shows that words have been left out of a quotation: \"The Earth is … a tiny dot in a beam of sunlight.\" Don't change the meaning when you shorten a quote. In stories and dialogue, an ellipsis shows a pause, hesitation or unfinished thought: \"I think … no, I'm not sure.\"\none dash: break before an explanation or emphasis\ntwo dashes: nonessential information in the middle\ndash … dash (never dash … comma)\nellipsis (…): omitted words in a quotation • a pause or unfinished thought","examples":["The divers had one goal—to reach the bottom.","The Challenger Deep—the deepest known point in the ocean—is nearly 11,000 meters deep.","Three explorers—Piccard, Walsh and Cameron—have become famous for reaching it.","The original quote: \"The ocean, which covers most of our planet, is still largely unexplored.\" Shortened: \"The ocean … is still largely unexplored.\"","\"We're running out of … air,\" the diver whispered.","Space is silent—completely silent.","The rover—which landed in 2021—has found ancient lake beds.","\"I wonder…\" Adam said, staring at the stars."],"common_errors":["✗ The probe—launched in 1977, is still working. → ✓ The probe—launched in 1977—is still working. (Match the dashes.)","✗ Mars, the Red Planet—has two small moons. → ✓ Mars—the Red Planet—has two small moons. / Mars, the Red Planet, has two small moons. (Use matching marks.)","✗ The submarine dived to—the bottom of the trench. → ✓ The submarine dived to the bottom of the trench. (No dash between a verb/preposition and its object.)","✗ \"The ocean is still largely unexplored\" (cutting \"which covers most of our planet\" with no mark) → ✓ \"The ocean … is still largely unexplored.\" (Show omitted words with an ellipsis.)"],"arabic":"الشرطة (—) تُظهر انقطاعًا قويًّا في الجملة: قبل شرح أو قائمة أو نهاية مؤكدة، أو في زوج حول معلومة غير ضرورية وسط الجملة.\nيجب أن تُغلق الشرطة بشرطة لا بفاصلة، وهذا سؤال شائع في SAT (Boundaries).\nعلامة الحذف (…) تُظهر كلمات محذوفة من اقتباس دون تغيير المعنى، أو توقفًا وترددًا في الحوار.\nفي الإنجليزية الأمريكية تُكتب الشرطة الطويلة عادة دون مسافات."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Deepest Point—Nearly 11,000 Meters…: Dashes and Ellipses”. Let’s learn dashes for a break, emphasis or added explanation; paired dashes around nonessential information (don't mix a dash with a comma); ellipses for omitted words in a quotation and for a pause or unfinished thought together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U5-LS4 | The Deepest Point—Nearly 11,000 Meters…: Dashes and Ellipses","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"A dash (—) shows a strong break in a sentence. Use one dash before an explanation, a list or an emphatic ending: They needed one thing to survive—oxygen. Use a pair of dashes around nonessential information in the middle of a sentence: Voyager 1—launched in 1977—is still working.","on_screen":"dashes for a break, emphasis or added explanation; paired dashes around nonessential information (don't mix a dash with a comma); ellipses for omitted words in a quotation and for a pause or unfinished thought","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A dash (—) shows a strong break in a sentence. Use one dash before an explanation, a list or an emphatic ending: They needed one thing to survive—oxygen. Use a pair of dashes around nonessential information in the middle of a sentence: Voyager 1—launched in 1977—is still working. Use matching punctuation: a dash must be closed by a dash, not a comma ((not: The probe—launched in 1977, is still working.)). This is a common SAT Boundaries question. In American English, the long dash usually has no spaces.","on_screen":"one dash: break before an explanation or emphasis • two dashes: nonessential information in the middle • dash … dash (never dash … comma)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The divers had one goal—to reach the bottom. The Challenger Deep—the deepest known point in the ocean—is nearly 11,000 meters deep. Three explorers—Piccard, Walsh and Cameron—have become famous for reaching it.","on_screen":"The divers had one goal—to reach the bottom. / The Challenger Deep—the deepest known point in the ocean—is nearly 11,000 meters deep. / Three explorers—Piccard, Walsh and Cameron—have become famous for reaching it.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The probe—launched in 1977, is still working.” That’s not right. We say: “The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.” Match the dashes.","on_screen":"✗ The probe—launched in 1977, is still working.   ✓ The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"The probe—launched in 1977, is still working.\" What's wrong? … Think! … The second mark should be a dash. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: dashes for a break, emphasis or added explanation; paired dashes around nonessential information (don't mix a dash with a comma); ellipses for omitted words in a quotation and for a pause or unfinished thought.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الشرطة (—) تُظهر انقطاعًا قويًّا في الجملة: قبل شرح أو قائمة أو نهاية مؤكدة، أو في زوج حول معلومة غير ضرورية وسط الجملة.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The probe—launched in 1977, is still working. — الصحيح: The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب The probe—launched in 1977—is still working."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Deepest Dive (editing passage)","body":"On January 23, 1960, two explorers,Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh,reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep. Their vesselthe Trieste,was built to survive enormous pressure. When they reached the bottom, they saw something surprising,a flat fish. For the next fifty years nobody returned to that spot.","word_count":45,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","items":[{"q":"","options":["The probe, launched in 1977—is still working.","The probe—launched in 1977, is still working.","The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.","The probe—launched in 1977 is still working."],"answer":"The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.","explanation":"Don't mix a dash with a comma.","explanation_ar":"لا خلط بين الشرطة والفاصلة."},{"q":"","options":["The divers needed—one thing to survive oxygen.","The divers needed one thing to survive—oxygen.","The divers—needed one thing to survive oxygen.","The divers needed one thing—to survive oxygen."],"answer":"The divers needed one thing to survive—oxygen.","explanation":"One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"","options":["Three explorers—Piccard, Walsh and Cameron—have reached the Challenger Deep.","Three—explorers Piccard, Walsh and Cameron have reached the Challenger Deep.","Three explorers Piccard, Walsh and Cameron—have reached the Challenger Deep.","Three explorers—Piccard, Walsh and Cameron, have reached the Challenger Deep."],"answer":"Three explorers—Piccard, Walsh and Cameron—have reached the Challenger Deep.","explanation":"Paired dashes around nonessential information.","explanation_ar":"زوج الشرطات."},{"q":"Why is an ellipsis used in \"The ocean … is still largely unexplored\"?","options":["It ends a question.","The speaker is shouting.","It joins two sentences.","Some words were left out of the quotation."],"answer":"Some words were left out of the quotation.","explanation":"Ellipsis for omitted words in a quotation.","explanation_ar":"الحذف من الاقتباس."},{"q":"Why is an ellipsis used in \"I think … no, I'm not sure\"?","options":["to show a pause or hesitation","to end the sentence","to show a list","to show omitted words in a book title"],"answer":"to show a pause or hesitation","explanation":"Ellipsis for a pause or unfinished thought.","explanation_ar":"التوقف والتردد."},{"q":"The Challenger Deep ______ is located in the Mariana Trench in the western Pacific Ocean.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["the deepest known point in Earth's oceans—",", the deepest known point in Earth's oceans—","—the deepest known point in Earth's oceans—","—the deepest known point in Earth's oceans,"],"answer":"—the deepest known point in Earth's oceans—","explanation":"Don't mix a dash with a comma.","explanation_ar":"لا خلط بين الشرطة والفاصلة."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Add dashes or an ellipsis.","items":[{"q":"Space is silent completely silent. (dash)","options":[],"answer":"Space is silent—completely silent.","explanation":"One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"The rover which landed in 2021 has found ancient lake beds. (two dashes)","options":[],"answer":"The rover—which landed in 2021—has found ancient lake beds.","explanation":"Paired dashes around nonessential information.","explanation_ar":"زوج الشرطات."},{"q":"Shorten the quotation by leaving out \"which covers most of our planet\": \"The ocean, which covers most of our planet, is still unexplored.\"  (ellipsis)","options":[],"answer":"\"The ocean … is still unexplored.\"","explanation":"Ellipsis for omitted words in a quotation.","explanation_ar":"الحذف من الاقتباس."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Mars—the Red Planet,has two small moons.","options":[],"answer":"Mars—the Red Planet—has two small moons.","explanation":"Planet, → Planet—. Don't mix a dash with a comma.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Planet, → Planet—. لا خلط بين الشرطة والفاصلة."},{"q":"The submarine dived to—the bottom of the trench.","options":[],"answer":"The submarine dived to the bottom of the trench.","explanation":"to—the bottom → to the bottom. One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: to—the bottom → to the bottom. الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"The astronaut—who spent a year in space has written a book.","options":[],"answer":"The astronaut—who spent a year in space—has written a book.","explanation":"space has → space—has. Paired dashes around nonessential information.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: space has → space—has. زوج الشرطات."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the expedition report.","items":[{"q":"On January 23, 1960, two explorers,Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh,reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep.","options":[],"answer":"On January 23, 1960, two explorers—Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh—reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep.","explanation":", → —. One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → —. الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"On January 23, 1960, two explorers,Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh,reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep.","options":[],"answer":"On January 23, 1960, two explorers—Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh—reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep.","explanation":", → —. One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → —. الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"Their vesselthe Trieste—was built to survive enormous pressure.","options":[],"answer":"Their vessel—the Trieste—was built to survive enormous pressure.","explanation":"(add) → —. One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (add) → —. الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"On January 23, 1960, two explorers,Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh,reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep.","options":[],"answer":"On January 23, 1960, two explorers—Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh—reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep.","explanation":", → —. One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → —. الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"On January 23, 1960, two explorers,Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh,reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep.","options":[],"answer":"On January 23, 1960, two explorers—Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh—reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep.","explanation":", → —. One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → —. الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"For the next fifty years nobody returned to that spot.","options":[],"answer":"For the next fifty years, nobody returned to that spot.","explanation":"(add) → ,. One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (add) → ,. الشرطة الواحدة."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write with dashes and ellipses.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with one dash before an emphatic ending about space or the ocean.","options":[],"answer":"The ocean floor is home to creatures we have never seen—not even in photos.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses dashes for a break, emphasis or added explanation; paired dashes around nonessential information (don't mix a dash with a comma); ellipses for omitted words in a quotation and for a pause or unfinished thought correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with a pair of dashes around extra information.","options":[],"answer":"The Red Sea—one of the saltiest seas in the world—has beautiful coral reefs.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses dashes for a break, emphasis or added explanation; paired dashes around nonessential information (don't mix a dash with a comma); ellipses for omitted words in a quotation and for a pause or unfinished thought correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a short line of dialogue with an ellipsis to show hesitation.","options":[],"answer":"\"Is that … a shark?\" Lina whispered.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses dashes for a break, emphasis or added explanation; paired dashes around nonessential information (don't mix a dash with a comma); ellipses for omitted words in a quotation and for a pause or unfinished thought correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spelling Relay","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["Teams line up facing the board.","The teacher says a word; the first student runs to the board and writes the first letter.","The next student adds the next letter, and so on.","The first team with the correct spelling wins the point."],"data":[{"word":"voyage","clue":"a long journey, especially by sea or in space"},{"word":"navigate","clue":"to find the right direction to travel"},{"word":"terrain","clue":"an area of land and its physical features"},{"word":"atmosphere","clue":"the layer of gases around a planet"},{"word":"submersible","clue":"a small vessel that can go deep under water"},{"word":"uncharted","clue":"not yet explored or shown on a map"},{"word":"probe","clue":"a spacecraft without people that is sent to explore space"},{"word":"orbit","clue":"to move in a circle around a planet, moon or star"},{"word":"launch","clue":"to send a rocket or spacecraft into the sky or space"},{"word":"spacecraft","clue":"a vehicle that can travel in space"},{"word":"lunar","clue":"connected with the Moon"},{"word":"marine","clue":"connected with the sea"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["The rover which weighs—about one ton moves very slowly.","The rover, which weighs about one ton—moves very slowly.","The rover—which weighs about one ton—moves very slowly.","The rover—which weighs about one ton, moves very slowly."],"answer":"The rover—which weighs about one ton—moves very slowly.","explanation":"Don't mix a dash with a comma.","explanation_ar":"لا خلط بين الشرطة والفاصلة."},{"q":"","options":["Only—one word described the view from space breathtaking.","Only one word—described the view from space breathtaking.","Only one word described the view from space—breathtaking.","Only one word described—the view from space breathtaking."],"answer":"Only one word described the view from space—breathtaking.","explanation":"One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"Where should the second dash go? \"The Moon—Earth's only natural satellite is moving slowly away from us.\"","options":["after \"satellite\"","after \"us\"","after \"slowly\"","after \"Moon\""],"answer":"after \"satellite\"","explanation":"Paired dashes around nonessential information.","explanation_ar":"زوج الشرطات."},{"q":"","options":["\"We need to—wait did you hear that.\"","\"We need … to wait did you … hear that.\"","\"We need to … wait, did you hear that?\"","\"We need to, wait, did you hear that?\""],"answer":"\"We need to … wait, did you hear that?\"","explanation":"Ellipsis for a pause or unfinished thought.","explanation_ar":"التوقف والتردد."},{"q":"Which shortened quotation uses an ellipsis correctly? Original: \"The International Space Station, which was first occupied in 2000, has hosted astronauts from many countries.\"","options":["\"The International … Station which hosted.\"","\"The International Space Station has … from many countries astronauts.\"","\"The International Space Station, … , has hosted.\"","\"The International Space Station … has hosted astronauts from many countries.\""],"answer":"\"The International Space Station … has hosted astronauts from many countries.\"","explanation":"Ellipsis for omitted words in a quotation.","explanation_ar":"الحذف من الاقتباس."},{"q":"Which mark gives the strongest break before an explanation?","options":["a dash","a hyphen","a comma","an apostrophe"],"answer":"a dash","explanation":"One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"","options":["The explorers carried everything they needed, food, water—oxygen and tools.","The explorers—carried everything they needed food, water, oxygen and tools.","The explorers carried—everything—they needed food, water, oxygen and tools.","The explorers carried everything they needed—food, water, oxygen and tools."],"answer":"The explorers carried everything they needed—food, water, oxygen and tools.","explanation":"One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"In 2012, the filmmaker James Cameron ______ made the first solo dive to the bottom of the Challenger Deep.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["who is also a deep-sea explorer—","—who is also a deep-sea explorer—","—who is also a deep-sea explorer,",", who is also a deep-sea explorer—"],"answer":"—who is also a deep-sea explorer—","explanation":"Don't mix a dash with a comma.","explanation_ar":"لا خلط بين الشرطة والفاصلة."},{"q":"The Empty Quarter ______ covers parts of Saudi Arabia, Oman, the United Arab Emirates and Yemen.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["—one of the largest sand deserts in the world—","one of the largest sand deserts in the world—","—one of the largest sand deserts in the world,",", one of the largest sand deserts in the world—"],"answer":"—one of the largest sand deserts in the world—","explanation":"Paired dashes around nonessential information.","explanation_ar":"زوج الشرطات."},{"q":"What does the ellipsis show in \"I'm not sure we should go down there…\"?","options":["omitted words from a book","an unfinished thought","a question","a list of items"],"answer":"an unfinished thought","explanation":"Ellipsis for a pause or unfinished thought.","explanation_ar":"التوقف والتردد."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Exploration” that shows dashes for a break, emphasis or added explanation; paired dashes around nonessential information (don't mix a dash with a comma); ellipses for omitted words in a quotation and for a pause or unfinished thought in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Exploration” that shows dashes for a break, emphasis or added explanation; paired dashes around nonessential information (don't mix a dash with a comma); ellipses for omitted words in a quotation and for a pause or unfinished thought in action"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spelling Relay”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spelling Relay"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Deepest Point—Nearly 11,000 Meters…: Dashes and Ellipses”. Let’s learn dashes for a break, emphasis or added explanation; paired dashes around nonessential information (don't mix a dash with a comma); ellipses for omitted words in a quotation and for a pause or unfinished thought together. A dash (—) shows a strong break in a sentence. Use one dash before an explanation, a list or an emphatic ending: They needed one thing to survive—oxygen. Use a pair of dashes around nonessential information in the middle of a sentence: Voyager 1—launched in 1977—is still working. A dash (—) shows a strong break in a sentence. Use one dash before an explanation, a list or an emphatic ending: They needed one thing to survive—oxygen. Use a pair of dashes around nonessential information in the middle of a sentence: Voyager 1—launched in 1977—is still working. Use matching punctuation: a dash must be closed by a dash, not a comma ((not: The probe—launched in 1977, is still working.)). This is a common SAT Boundaries question. In American English, the long dash usually has no spaces. Here are some examples. The divers had one goal—to reach the bottom. The Challenger Deep—the deepest known point in the ocean—is nearly 11,000 meters deep. Three explorers—Piccard, Walsh and Cameron—have become famous for reaching it. Be careful! Some learners say: “The probe—launched in 1977, is still working.” That’s not right. We say: “The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.” Match the dashes. Quick check: \"The probe—launched in 1977, is still working.\" What's wrong? … Think! … The second mark should be a dash. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The probe—launched in 1977—is still working.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS5","title":"The Deepest Dive: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to present an exploration mission using precise vocabulary and concise sentences.","Students will be able to write an exploration report with participle phrases, formal relative clauses, dashes and ellipses, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can present a famous expedition.","I can write a concise exploration report."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تقديم مهمة استكشافية بمفردات دقيقة وجمل موجزة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة تقرير استكشافي بعبارات اسم الفاعل والمفعول وجمل الوصل الرسمية والشرطات وعلامات الحذف، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أقدّم بعثة استكشافية مشهورة.","أستطيع أن أكتب تقريرًا استكشافيًّا موجزًا."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"voyage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɔɪədʒ/","definition":"a long journey, especially by sea or in space","arabic":"رحلة طويلة","example":"The voyage across the ocean took three months.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"probe","pos":"noun","ipa":"/proʊb/","definition":"a spacecraft without people that is sent to explore space","arabic":"مسبار","example":"The probe sent photos of Saturn back to Earth.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"orbit","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈɔrbət/","definition":"to move in a circle around a planet, moon or star","arabic":"يدور في مدار","example":"The International Space Station orbits the Earth every 90 minutes.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"launch","pos":"verb","ipa":"/lɔntʃ/","definition":"to send a rocket or spacecraft into the sky or space","arabic":"يُطلق","example":"The rocket was launched from Florida.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"spacecraft","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈspeɪˌskræft/","definition":"a vehicle that can travel in space","arabic":"مركبة فضائية","example":"The spacecraft landed safely on the Moon.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"submersible","pos":"noun","ipa":"/səbˈmɝsɪbəl/","definition":"a small vessel that can go deep under water","arabic":"غوّاصة صغيرة / مركبة غاطسة","example":"The submersible reached the bottom of the ocean.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"navigate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈnævəˌɡeɪt/","definition":"to find the right direction to travel","arabic":"يُبحر / يهتدي إلى الاتجاه","example":"Ancient sailors navigated by the stars.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"terrain","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tɚˈeɪn/","definition":"an area of land and its physical features","arabic":"تضاريس","example":"The rover crossed rocky terrain on Mars.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"lunar","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈlunɚ/","definition":"connected with the Moon","arabic":"قمري","example":"The lunar surface is covered in dust.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"marine","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/mɚˈin/","definition":"connected with the sea","arabic":"بحري","example":"Marine biologists study life in the ocean.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"uncharted","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ənˈtʃɑrtɪd/","definition":"not yet explored or shown on a map","arabic":"غير مستكشَف / مجهول","example":"Much of the deep ocean is still uncharted.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"frontier","pos":"noun","ipa":"/frənˈtɪr/","definition":"the edge of what is known or explored","arabic":"آفاق جديدة / تخوم","example":"Space is often called the final frontier.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"astronaut","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæstrəˌnɑt/","definition":"a person who travels into space","arabic":"رائد فضاء","example":"The astronaut spent six months on the space station.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"atmosphere","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈætməˌsfɪr/","definition":"the layer of gases around a planet","arabic":"الغلاف الجوي","example":"Mars has a very thin atmosphere.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"hostile","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈhɑstəl/","definition":"very difficult or dangerous to live in","arabic":"معادٍ / قاسٍ","example":"The deep sea is a hostile environment for humans.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 5: reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, and dashes and ellipses.\nGood reports are precise and concise. Participle phrases and reduced clauses remove extra words; formal relative clauses sound academic; dashes add emphasis; ellipses shorten quotations honestly. Transitions such as however, as a result, for example and meanwhile guide the reader through events.\n✔ Built in…, / Descending…, / Having reached…,\n✔ the cabin in which… / the explorer for whom…\n✔ —paired dashes— • \"quotation … shortened\"\n✔ voyage, probe, terrain, uncharted, hostile, frontier","examples":["Designed to withstand huge pressure, the vessel had very thick walls.","Scientists studying the deep sea have found glowing fish.","The cabin in which they sat was only two meters wide.","The engineer to whom they owed their safety had tested every bolt.","The trench—longer than the Red Sea—runs through the western Pacific.","\"The ocean … remains a mystery,\" the scientist said.","Having surfaced safely, the explorers were greeted as heroes.","The hostile conditions made the dive extremely dangerous."],"common_errors":["✗ Descending slowly, the darkness surrounded the explorers. → ✓ Descending slowly, the explorers were surrounded by darkness. (The participle phrase must describe the subject.)","✗ The cabin in which they sat in was tiny. → ✓ The cabin in which they sat was tiny. (No double preposition.)","✗ The pressure—more than a thousand times greater, could crush a submarine. → ✓ The pressure—more than a thousand times greater—could crush a submarine. (Match the dashes.)"],"arabic":"نراجع جمل الوصل المختصرة وعبارات اسم الفاعل والمفعول وحرف الجر + which/whom ومفردات الاستكشاف والجذور والشرطات وعلامات الحذف.\nالتقرير الجيد دقيق وموجز: عبارات اسم الفاعل تختصر الكلمات، وجمل الوصل الرسمية تبدو أكاديمية، والشرطات تُبرز المعلومة، وعلامات الحذف تختصر الاقتباس بأمانة.\nالروابط مثل however وas a result وfor example وmeanwhile ترشد القارئ عبر الأحداث."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Deepest Dive: Language in Use”. Let’s learn reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L08-U5-LS5 | The Deepest Dive: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Explorer press conference: groups choose an expedition (Voyager 1, the Trieste dive, the first Saudi in space, Ibn Battuta's journeys). One student is the explorer; others are reporters. Reporters ask 4 questions; the explorer answers using one participle phrase, one formal relative clause and one dash.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 5: reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, and dashes and ellipses. Good reports are precise and concise. Participle phrases and reduced clauses remove extra words; formal relative clauses sound academic; dashes add emphasis; ellipses shorten quotations honestly. Transitions such as however, as a result, for example and meanwhile guide the reader through events.","on_screen":"✔ Built in…, / Descending…, / Having reached…, • ✔ the cabin in which… / the explorer for whom… • ✔ —paired dashes— • \"quotation … shortened\"","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Designed to withstand huge pressure, the vessel had very thick walls. Scientists studying the deep sea have found glowing fish. The cabin in which they sat was only two meters wide.","on_screen":"Designed to withstand huge pressure, the vessel had very thick walls. / Scientists studying the deep sea have found glowing fish. / The cabin in which they sat was only two meters wide.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Descending slowly, the darkness surrounded the explorers.” That’s not right. We say: “Descending slowly, the explorers were surrounded by darkness.” The participle phrase must describe the subject.","on_screen":"✗ Descending slowly, the darkness surrounded the explorers.   ✓ Descending slowly, the explorers were surrounded by darkness.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “The cabin in which they sat in was tiny.”? … Think! … It should be “The cabin in which they sat was tiny.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present a famous expedition.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can present a famous expedition.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع جمل الوصل المختصرة وعبارات اسم الفاعل والمفعول وحرف الجر + which/whom ومفردات الاستكشاف والجذور والشرطات وعلامات الحذف.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Descending slowly, the darkness surrounded the explorers. — الصحيح: Descending slowly, the explorers were surrounded by darkness.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقدّم بعثة استكشافية مشهورة."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"The Deepest Dive – Noura","body":"Built in Italy in 1953, the Trieste was a strange-looking vessel designed for one purpose—to reach the deepest parts of the ocean.\nOn January 23, 1960, Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh climbed into the tiny cabin in which they would spend almost nine hours.\nDescending slowly through total darkness, they reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep, nearly 11,000 meters below the surface.\nThe pressure at that depth—more than a thousand times greater than at sea level—could crush an ordinary submarine.\nPiccard later described seeing a flat fish on the seafloor, a discovery that surprised scientists studying life in extreme environments.\nFifty-two years later, the filmmaker James Cameron, for whom the ocean had always been a passion, made the first solo dive to the same spot.\nAs our science teacher likes to say, \"The deep ocean … is the last great frontier on our own planet.\"","word_count":146,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Noura's report. True or False?","items":[{"q":"The Trieste was built in Italy.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “The Deepest Dive – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Piccard and Walsh reached the bottom in 1953.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “The Deepest Dive – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The dive took almost nine hours.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “The Deepest Dive – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The pressure at the bottom is lower than at sea level.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “The Deepest Dive – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Piccard said he saw a flat fish on the seafloor.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “The Deepest Dive – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"James Cameron made his dive with two other people.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “The Deepest Dive – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ in Italy in 1953, the Trieste was designed to reach the deepest parts of the ocean.","options":["It was built","Building","Having build","Built"],"answer":"Built","explanation":"Participle phrase or reduced relative clause.","explanation_ar":"عبارة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول."},{"q":"They climbed into the tiny cabin ___ they would spend almost nine hours.","options":["in whom","in that","in which","which in"],"answer":"in which","explanation":"Preposition + which/whom.","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر + which/whom."},{"q":"Cameron, ___ the ocean had always been a passion, made a solo dive.","options":["whom for","for whom","for which","for who"],"answer":"for whom","explanation":"Preposition + which/whom.","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر + which/whom."},{"q":"","options":["The pressure, more than a thousand times greater than at sea level—could crush a submarine.","The pressure more than a thousand times greater—than at sea level could crush a submarine.","The pressure—more than a thousand times greater than at sea level, could crush a submarine.","The pressure—more than a thousand times greater than at sea level—could crush a submarine."],"answer":"The pressure—more than a thousand times greater than at sea level—could crush a submarine.","explanation":"Dashes and ellipses.","explanation_ar":"الشرطات وعلامات الحذف."},{"q":"Scientists ___ life in extreme environments were surprised by the flat fish.","options":["are studying","studied","studying","who studying"],"answer":"studying","explanation":"Participle phrase or reduced relative clause.","explanation_ar":"عبارة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول."},{"q":"The Trieste reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep in 1960. ______ no human returned to that spot for more than fifty years.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","For example,","Similarly,","Therefore,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Prepare for the press conference.","items":[{"q":"Your expedition and the explorer you play: ___","options":[],"answer":"Voyager 1 – I play a NASA engineer.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence with a participle phrase: ___","options":[],"answer":"Launched in 1977, our probe has traveled farther than any other human-made object.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence with a formal relative clause: ___","options":[],"answer":"The team with which I worked had only twenty engineers.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence with a pair of dashes: ___","options":[],"answer":"Our biggest challenge—keeping the probe powered—gets harder every year.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A question a reporter could ask with \"to whom\" or \"for which\": ___","options":[],"answer":"To whom did you send the first message from the probe?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your exploration report.","items":[{"q":"Plan: expedition, date, people, vessel or vehicle, challenge, discovery, quotation.","options":[],"answer":"first Saudi in space; June 1985; Prince Sultan bin Salman; Space Shuttle Discovery; seven days in orbit; photos of the Arabian Peninsula; a quotation from our science teacher","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your exploration report in 8–9 sentences.","options":[],"answer":"In June 1985, Prince Sultan bin Salman became the first Arab and the first Muslim to travel into space. Launched from Florida on the Space Shuttle Discovery, he spent seven days in orbit with an international crew. The mission—only a week long—made history for the whole Arab world. Looking down at Earth, he photographed the Arabian Peninsula. The crew with whom he flew included astronauts from the United States and France. His journey inspired young people studying science across the region. Many years later, in 2023, Saudi astronauts traveled to the International Space Station. As our science teacher told us, \"Every journey into space … begins with a question in a classroom.\"","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: participle phrases with the right subject? no double prepositions? dashes in pairs? ellipsis used honestly? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"Looking down at Earth, the Arabian Peninsula was photographed\" to \"Looking down at Earth, he photographed the Arabian Peninsula.\"","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Board Race Review","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["One student from each team stands at the board.","The teacher says a cue word and a task (meaning, spelling or sentence with reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review)).","The first correct answer wins a point; rotate players every round.","Play 10 rounds."],"data":[{"cue":"voyage","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"probe","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"orbit","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"launch","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"spacecraft","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"submersible","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"navigate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"terrain","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"lunar","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"marine","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"uncharted","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"frontier","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"astronaut","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"atmosphere","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"hostile","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___ by a team of engineers, the rover can work in extreme temperatures.","options":["Having design","It was designed","Designed","Designing"],"answer":"Designed","explanation":"Participle phrase or reduced relative clause.","explanation_ar":"عبارة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول."},{"q":"Astronauts ___ on the space station grow vegetables in special chambers.","options":["who living","living","lived","are living"],"answer":"living","explanation":"Participle phrase or reduced relative clause.","explanation_ar":"عبارة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول."},{"q":"The scientist ___ the discovery is credited works at KAUST.","options":["to which","to who","to whom","whom to"],"answer":"to whom","explanation":"Preposition + which/whom.","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر + which/whom."},{"q":"The trench ___ the deepest point lies is in the Pacific Ocean.","options":["in that","which in","in whom","in which"],"answer":"in which","explanation":"Preposition + which/whom.","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر + which/whom."},{"q":"Much of the ocean floor is still ___.","options":["uncharted","lunar","orbital","navigated"],"answer":"uncharted","explanation":"Exploration word or root.","explanation_ar":"كلمة الاستكشاف."},{"q":"","options":["The submarine—built to survive enormous pressure, reached the seafloor.","The submarine—built to survive enormous pressure—reached the seafloor.","The submarine built—to survive enormous pressure reached the seafloor.","The submarine, built to survive enormous pressure—reached the seafloor."],"answer":"The submarine—built to survive enormous pressure—reached the seafloor.","explanation":"Dashes and ellipses.","explanation_ar":"الشرطات وعلامات الحذف."},{"q":"Space travel is extremely expensive. ______ a single launch of a large rocket can cost hundreds of millions of dollars.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Instead,","In contrast,","For example,"],"answer":"For example,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"Early explorers navigated using the stars and the Sun. ______ modern ships use satellites to find their exact position.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For example,","As a result,","Today,","Similarly,"],"answer":"Today,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Rayyanah Barnawi is a Saudi biomedical scientist.\n• In May 2023, she traveled to the International Space Station.\n• She became the first Saudi woman in space.\n• On the station, she did experiments on stem cells.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize the historic importance of Barnawi's mission. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Experiments on stem cells were done on the space station.","The International Space Station orbits the Earth.","Rayyanah Barnawi is a biomedical scientist from Saudi Arabia.","In May 2023, Rayyanah Barnawi made history as the first Saudi woman to travel to space."],"answer":"In May 2023, Rayyanah Barnawi made history as the first Saudi woman to travel to space.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Voyager 1 was launched in 1977.\n• In 2012, it entered interstellar space.\n• It is the most distant human-made object.\n• It carries a golden record with sounds from Earth.\n\nThe student wants to describe how far Voyager 1 has traveled. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Having entered interstellar space in 2012, Voyager 1 is now the most distant human-made object.","The golden record on Voyager 1 is famous.","Voyager 1 was launched in 1977 from Florida.","Voyager 1 carries a golden record with sounds from Earth."],"answer":"Having entered interstellar space in 2012, Voyager 1 is now the most distant human-made object.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A cross-section of the ocean from the surface to the Challenger Deep, with a tiny bathyscaphe Trieste descending into darkness, glowing deep-sea creatures, and a depth scale reaching nearly 11,000 meters; Noura presenting the poster. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A cross-section of the ocean from the surface to the Challenger Deep, with a tiny bathyscaphe Trieste descending into darkness, glowing deep-sea creatures, and a depth scale reaching nearly 11,000 meters; Noura presenting the poster"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Exploration”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Exploration”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L08-U5-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Board Race Review”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Board Race Review"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS5-T1","script":"Built in Italy in 1953, the Trieste was a strange-looking vessel designed for one purpose—to reach the deepest parts of the ocean. On January 23, 1960, Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh climbed into the tiny cabin in which they would spend almost nine hours. Descending slowly through total darkness, they reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep, nearly 11,000 meters below the surface. The pressure at that depth—more than a thousand times greater than at sea level—could crush an ordinary submarine. Piccard later described seeing a flat fish on the seafloor, a discovery that surprised scientists studying life in extreme environments. Fifty-two years later, the filmmaker James Cameron, for whom the ocean had always been a passion, made the first solo dive to the same spot. As our science teacher likes to say, \"The deep ocean … is the last great frontier on our own planet.\"","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L08-U5-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Deepest Dive: Language in Use”. Let’s learn reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, dashes and ellipses; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Explorer press conference: groups choose an expedition (Voyager 1, the Trieste dive, the first Saudi in space, Ibn Battuta's journeys). One student is the explorer; others are reporters. Reporters ask 4 questions; the explorer answers using one participle phrase, one formal relative clause and one dash. Today you use all of Unit 5: reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, preposition + which/whom, exploration vocabulary and roots, and dashes and ellipses. Good reports are precise and concise. Participle phrases and reduced clauses remove extra words; formal relative clauses sound academic; dashes add emphasis; ellipses shorten quotations honestly. Transitions such as however, as a result, for example and meanwhile guide the reader through events. Here are some examples. Designed to withstand huge pressure, the vessel had very thick walls. Scientists studying the deep sea have found glowing fish. The cabin in which they sat was only two meters wide. Be careful! Some learners say: “Descending slowly, the darkness surrounded the explorers.” That’s not right. We say: “Descending slowly, the explorers were surrounded by darkness.” The participle phrase must describe the subject. Quick check: what is wrong with “The cabin in which they sat in was tiny.”? … Think! … It should be “The cabin in which they sat was tiny.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present a famous expedition.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"People ___ in coastal cities are worried about rising sea levels.","options":["lived","are living","who living","living"],"answer":"living","explanation":"Active reduced clause → -ing participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المعلومة ← ing."},{"q":"The samples ___ by the rover will be studied in a lab.","options":["are collected","collected","collecting","which collected"],"answer":"collected","explanation":"Passive reduced clause → past participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المجهولة ← التصريف الثالث."},{"q":"___ in 1990, the Hubble Space Telescope has changed our view of the universe.","options":["Launching","It was launched","Launched","Having launch"],"answer":"Launched","explanation":"Participle phrase at the start describing the subject.","explanation_ar":"العبارة في أول الجملة."},{"q":"___ the map carefully, the explorers chose the safest route.","options":["Having study","Has studied","Having studied","Studied"],"answer":"Having studied","explanation":"Earlier action → Having + past participle.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"","options":["Floating above the Earth, the astronaut took hundreds of photos.","Floating above the Earth, hundreds of photos were taken.","Floating above the Earth, the photos were amazing.","Floating above the Earth, the camera took hundreds of photos by itself."],"answer":"Floating above the Earth, the astronaut took hundreds of photos.","explanation":"The subject must match the participle phrase.","explanation_ar":"تطابق الفاعل."},{"q":"The Perseverance rover, ______ on Mars in February 2021, has been collecting rock samples that may contain signs of ancient life.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["that it landed","which landed","landing it","which it landed"],"answer":"which landed","explanation":"Passive reduced clause → past participle.","explanation_ar":"المختصرة المجهولة ← التصريف الثالث."},{"q":"The crater ___ the meteorite landed is now a tourist attraction.","options":["in that","which in","in whom","in which"],"answer":"in which","explanation":"Preposition + which for things.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + which."},{"q":"The teacher ___ we owe our love of science retired last year.","options":["to whom","to which","whom to","to who"],"answer":"to whom","explanation":"Preposition + whom for people.","explanation_ar":"حرف جر + whom."},{"q":"","options":["The rocket on which the satellite was carried on lifted off at dawn.","The rocket on that the satellite was carried lifted off at dawn.","The rocket on which the satellite was carried lifted off at dawn.","The rocket which on the satellite was carried lifted off at dawn."],"answer":"The rocket on which the satellite was carried lifted off at dawn.","explanation":"Don't repeat the preposition.","explanation_ar":"لا تكرار لحرف الجر."},{"q":"Ibn Battuta, ______ journeys took him from Morocco to China, wrote one of the most famous travel books in history.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["whom","whose","who's","which"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"whose for possession.","explanation_ar":"whose."},{"q":"The space ___ was sent to study Jupiter's moons.","options":["probe","frontier","terrain","voyage"],"answer":"probe","explanation":"Choose the exploration word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"\"Aqua\" in \"aquarium\" means ___.","options":["star","sun","water","land"],"answer":"water","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root.","explanation_ar":"الجذر."},{"q":"Early sailors relied on the stars for ___.","options":["navigation","navigate","navigator","navigable"],"answer":"navigation","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Without a thick ______ to protect it, the surface of Mars is exposed to dangerous radiation from the Sun.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["voyage","atmosphere","frontier","terrain"],"answer":"atmosphere","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The ___ surface is covered with craters made by meteorites.","options":["marine","solar","aquatic","lunar"],"answer":"lunar","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root.","explanation_ar":"الجذر."},{"q":"","options":["The Red Sea—one of the warmest seas on Earth, has amazing coral reefs.","The Red Sea, one of the warmest seas on Earth—has amazing coral reefs.","The Red Sea one of the warmest—seas on Earth has amazing coral reefs.","The Red Sea—one of the warmest seas on Earth—has amazing coral reefs."],"answer":"The Red Sea—one of the warmest seas on Earth—has amazing coral reefs.","explanation":"Don't mix a dash with a comma.","explanation_ar":"لا خلط بين الشرطة والفاصلة."},{"q":"","options":["The astronauts faced—one great danger the cold of space.","The astronauts faced one great—danger the cold of space.","The astronauts faced one great danger—the cold of space.","The astronauts—faced one great danger the cold of space."],"answer":"The astronauts faced one great danger—the cold of space.","explanation":"One dash for a break, explanation or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطة الواحدة."},{"q":"Why is an ellipsis used in \"Space … is the final frontier\"?","options":["Some words from the original quotation were left out.","It ends the sentence.","It shows a list.","The speaker forgot the words."],"answer":"Some words from the original quotation were left out.","explanation":"Ellipsis for omitted words in a quotation.","explanation_ar":"الحذف من الاقتباس."},{"q":"Hegra ______ was carved by the Nabataeans more than two thousand years ago.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[", Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site—","—Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site—","Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site—","—Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site,"],"answer":"—Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site—","explanation":"Paired dashes around nonessential information.","explanation_ar":"زوج الشرطات."},{"q":"The first humans landed on the Moon in 1969. ______ no one has walked on the Moon since 1972.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Therefore,","Similarly,","For example,","However,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• The Mariana Trench is in the western Pacific Ocean.\n• Its deepest point is the Challenger Deep.\n• The Challenger Deep is nearly 11,000 meters deep.\n• Mount Everest is about 8,849 meters high.\n\nThe student wants to help readers imagine how deep the Challenger Deep is. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["The Mariana Trench is in the western Pacific Ocean.","Mount Everest is a very high mountain in Asia.","At nearly 11,000 meters, the Challenger Deep is so deep that Mount Everest, about 8,849 meters high, could fit inside it with room to spare.","The Challenger Deep is the deepest point of the Mariana Trench."],"answer":"At nearly 11,000 meters, the Challenger Deep is so deep that Mount Everest, about 8,849 meters high, could fit inside it with room to spare.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Which sentence would fit best in an exploration report?","options":["Descended into the dark trench, the explorers watched glowing creatures—drift past.","The explorers descending into the dark trench, they watched glowing creatures.","Descending into the dark trench, glowing creatures drifted past the vessel in which they sat in.","Descending into the dark trench, the explorers watched glowing creatures drift past the window of the vessel in which they sat."],"answer":"Descending into the dark trench, the explorers watched glowing creatures drift past the window of the vessel in which they sat.","explanation":"Participle phrase at the start describing the subject.","explanation_ar":"العبارة في أول الجملة."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"the probe—sent to study saturn, discovered new moons.\"","options":["The probe—sent to study Saturn—discovered new moons.","The probe, sent to study Saturn—discovered new moons.","The probe—sent to study Saturn, discovered new moons.","the probe—sent to study Saturn—discovered new moons."],"answer":"The probe—sent to study Saturn—discovered new moons.","explanation":"Don't mix a dash with a comma.","explanation_ar":"لا خلط بين الشرطة والفاصلة."},{"q":"\"Having reached the summit, the climbers rested.\" Which action happened first?","options":["We can't tell.","reaching the summit","Both happened at the same time.","resting"],"answer":"reaching the summit","explanation":"Earlier action → Having + past participle.","explanation_ar":"Having done."}],"theme_ar":"الاستكشاف","essential_question_ar":"لماذا يواصل الإنسان استكشاف المجهول؟","can_do":"I can write concise, sophisticated sentences with reduced relative clauses and participle phrases, use preposition + which/whom, understand exploration words through Greek and Latin roots, and use dashes and ellipses correctly.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أكتب جملًا موجزة ومتقنة بجمل الوصل المختصرة وعبارات اسم الفاعل والمفعول، وأستخدم حرف الجر + which/whom، وأفهم كلمات الاستكشاف من خلال الجذور اليونانية واللاتينية، وأستخدم الشرطة وعلامة الحذف بشكل صحيح."});
